🔐 Trust Policy (Assume Role Policy)
👉 Answers:
“Who can assume this role?”

Used ONLY for:
IAM Roles

STS AssumeRole

Example:
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:user/devops-user"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
Means:
✔ IAM user devops-user
✔ Can assume this role
❌ No permissions yet

❗Important
A trust policy does NOT grant access to AWS services.

🛠️ Permission Policy
👉 Answers:
“What can this role do after it is assumed?”

Example:
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "ec2:RunInstances",
        "elasticloadbalancing:CreateLoadBalancer"
      ],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
Means:
✔ Create EC2
✔ Create ALB
❌ Still cannot assume role without trust policy

🔥 Key Difference Table
Feature	Trust Policy	Permission Policy
Used for	Who can assume	What actions allowed
Attached to	IAM Role only	User / Role / Group
Contains	Principal	Action, Resource
STS related	✅ Yes	❌ No
Controls AWS services	❌ No	✅ Yes
2️⃣ Why IAM Roles Have TWO POLICIES
This is by design for security.

🔒 Security Reason (MOST IMPORTANT)
AWS separates:

Identity authentication

Authorization

Step-by-Step Flow (VERY IMPORTANT)
IAM User / EC2 / Lambda
        |
        | 1️⃣ Trust Policy check
        ↓
IAM Role
        |
        | 2️⃣ Permission Policy check
        ↓
AWS Services (EC2, S3, ALB)
🔍 What Happens Internally
Step 1️⃣ Trust Policy
“Is this entity allowed to assume me?”

If ❌ → Access Denied

Step 2️⃣ Permission Policy
“What is this role allowed to do?”

If ❌ → Access Denied

❌ What If Only One Policy Existed?
Scenario	Risk
Anyone could assume role	🚨 Security breach
Assumer automatically gets permissions	🚨 Privilege escalation
Cross-account unsafe	🚨 Huge risk
